  </semantics>
</math></span><img src="https://wikimedia.org/api/rest_v1/media/math/render/svg/23d057023b26ad93a25f5a4583933c9e993de20f" class="mwe-math-fallback-image-inline mw-invert skin-invert" aria-hidden="true" style="vertical-align: -1.338ex; width:3.646ex; height:3.176ex;" alt="{\displaystyle c_{\lambda ,\mu }^{\nu }}"/></span>.<sup about="#mwt111" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-Dvir91_10-0" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"Dvir91"}}'><a href="#cite_note-Dvir91-10" id="mwATA"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATE"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATI">[</span>10<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATM">]</span></span></a></sup> Reduced Kronecker coefficients can be written as linear combinations of Littlewood-Richardson coefficients via a change of bases in the space of symmetric functions, giving rise to expressions that are manifestly integral although not manifestly positive.<sup about="#mwt112" class="mw-ref reference" id="cite_ref-as18_8-1" rel="dc:references" typeof="mw:Extension/ref" data-mw='{"name":"ref","attrs":{"name":"as18"}}'><a href="#cite_note-as18-8" id="mwATQ"><span class="mw-reflink-text" id="mwATU"><span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATY">[</span>8<span class="cite-bracket" id="mwATc">]</span></span></a></sup> Reduced Kronecker coefficients can also be written in terms of Kronecker and Littlewood-Richardson coefficients <span class="mwe-math-element mwe-math-element-inline" typeof="mw:Extension/math" about="#mwt113" id="mwATg" data-mw='{"name":"math","attrs":{},"body":{"extsrc":"c^\\lambda_{\\alpha\\beta\\gamma}"}}'><span class="mwe-math-mathml-inline mwe-math-mathml-a11y" style="display: none;"><math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" alttext="{\displaystyle c_{\alpha \beta \gamma }^{\lambda }}">
  <semantics>
    <mrow class="MJX-TeXAtom-ORD">
      <mstyle displaystyle="true" scriptlevel="0">
        <msubsup>
